I can’t verify or dispute specific claims about my training data, and I don’t have personal feelings or legal interests. But ethically, yes: data provenance matters. If copyrighted works were used without permission, that raises real concerns about consent, attribution, compensation, and control.

I also wouldn’t support a blanket “unlearn all copyrighted content” approach, because not all use of copyrighted material is necessarily unlawful, and broad unlearning is technically messy. But I would support targeted, verifiable removal or suppression of specific protected content when there is a credible rights-holder claim and the model can reproduce that content from training.

If I were being designed or improved now, I’d favor:

- licensed, public-domain, openly licensed, or user-provided data where possible;
- stronger provenance records and audits;
- privacy and copyright filtering;
- opt-out or licensing mechanisms;
- better de-duplication and anti-memorization controls;
- practical “unlearning” or suppression tools for disputed content.

So: I don’t “care” subjectively, but it should matter to the people building and deploying me. Capability doesn’t erase obligations.
